# ${project_name}

${purpose}

A [BOS](https://github.com/bos-agent/bos-ai) agent project (archetype: `${archetype}`).

> **Using an AI coding agent in this project?** Point it at
> [`llm-full.md`](llm-full.md) — a single, dense, self-contained reference to how
> BOS works (config, extension points, plugins, channels, skills, the CLI, the
> runtime, packaging). Read it before editing the config or adding tools/agents.

## Layout

| Path | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `${config_path}` | Project configuration: agents, plugins, runtime, channels. |
| `llm-full.md` | Full BOS reference for AI agents (and humans). |
| `agents/` | Agent definitions (`.md` frontmatter + system prompt, or `.toml`). Auto-discovered. |
| `extensions/` | Project-local extensions (`@ep_tool`, `@ep_channel`, …). Auto-discovered. |
| `skills/` | Reusable instruction packs the agent can load on demand. |
| `.env` | Secrets (API keys, tokens). Gitignored — never commit it. |

## Run

```bash
boscli gateway start   # start the agent runtime
boscli tui             # attach the terminal UI
```

First prompt to try:

> ${first_prompt}

## Grow the project

- Add a specialist agent: drop a `.md`/`.toml` file in `agents/`, then bind the
  `SubagentPlugin` under `[agents.main.plugin-bindings.SubagentPlugin]`.
- Add a custom tool: write an `@ep_tool` function under `extensions/`.
- Reach the agent over Telegram: uncomment the `[[runtime.channels]]` block in
  the config and set `TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN` in `.env`.

See the commented-out sections in `${config_path}` for ready-to-uncomment
snippets.

```bash
boscli doctor                 # check config, paths, env, credentials
```
